{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head 7a4d4336239ba472f4df8c51220b699c40065e78 on task/status-fast-usable.\n\nGates at this head: go vet ./... clean; go test ./... green across all thirteen packages; go test -race ./... green; go build ./... ok; UI 34 of 34 and npm run build; git diff --check clean; checkout clean at the exact head; current main edb53ce is an ancestor.\n\nI probed --server hardest, as the conditions asked, and it holds.\n\nThe transport guards are all present and each does something. validateLoopbackServer accepts only an http URL with a loopback host or localhost, and rejects credentials, query, fragment, and any path beyond \"/\". boundedClient refuses every redirect, so a compromised resident cannot bounce the CLI off-host. The summary path is capped at 64 KiB and two seconds, the full path at 64 MiB and ten seconds, with the limit enforced by reading limit+1 and comparing, so a response exactly at the boundary is accepted and one byte more is refused. Decoding uses DisallowUnknownFields and then requires io.EOF on a second decode, so neither an unexpected field nor trailing JSON passes.\n\nThe trust boundary is the part that matters, and it is real rather than decorative. fetchSummary reads the local ref head before the request, and validateRemoteFrontierAt then requires three things: the resident's genesis equals the selected workroom's configured genesis, the local head did not move during the read, and the resident's head equals that local head. The resident therefore cannot describe a frontier that local git does not already hold, and the cursor frontier must agree with the durable frontier it accompanies. I mutation-tested it: deleting only the \"resident summary head is not current\" check makes TestFetchSummaryPinsGenesisHeadCursorAndResponseCap fail. The check is load-bearing.\n\nLabelling is honest. Resident-sourced output renders \"(resident summary)\", locally audited output \"(verified local)\", and the post-failure path \"(verified local fallback)\". Every guard failure is named on stderr before the fallback runs, so a reader is told why they are seeing local work rather than silently given it. Nothing labels resident content as verified.\n\nThe 240-byte cap is a true UTF-8 byte ceiling, not a byte slice. Text reserves room for the ellipsis and then walks back to a rune start. I probed it exhaustively at this head, over 1, 2, 3 and 4-byte runes at every length from 1 to 400: the result never exceeds TextCap and is always valid UTF-8. Raising the constant to 4096 fails TestBuildBoundsEveryGrowthDimensionAndCountsExactly, so the cap is pinned rather than incidental.\n\ngs verify still performs a full audit, structurally and not by convention: Workspace.Verify calls kernel.Verify directly with no CheckpointOptions, so the checkpoint shortcut is not reachable from it. docs/reference.md now says exactly that, alongside the guards and the fact that local status consumes a sequencer-signed checkpoint and verifies its descendant tail.\n\nThe statusview extraction is a real simplification, not a move: spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp/digest.go loses 529 lines to a shared package that MCP, the service, and the CLI now all read, and /v0/status-summary is a thin bounded projection over the existing status rather than a second source of truth.\n\nTwo observations, neither blocking and neither a finding.\n\nFirst, fetchFullStatus does not take the before-read head that fetchSummary does; it validates only that the returned head equals the local head at validation time. That is still sufficient — a resident returning a head local git does not hold is refused either way — and the asymmetry only makes the summary path the stricter of the two. Worth knowing rather than changing.\n\nSecond, SummaryStatus embeds nexus.Snapshot whole, so Live.Presence and Live.Conversations are not bounded by statusview.Build the way every durable list is. A workroom with very many live leases could push the summary past the 64 KiB client cap, at which point the CLI discloses the oversize and falls back to a verified local read. That degrades gracefully and honestly, so it is correct today; if leases ever grow with instance identities, the cap is the place that will notice first.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2b2cafd2580ade35d92701f2e1b0139b228410d8","head":"7a4d4336239ba472f4df8c51220b699c40065e78","verdict":"approved"}}